C# TypeType .NETData representedLiteral suffixEncodingValue range[char][Char] (S)character 2 bytesUnicode character (UTF-16)[string][String] (C)character string reference to a Unicode character sequence[int][Int32] (S)integer 4 bytes[-2³¹, 2³¹-1] or [-2147483648, 2147483647][uint][UInt32] (S)unsigned integerU4 bytes[0, 2³²-1] or [0, 4294967295][long][Int64] (S)integerL8 bytes[-2⁶³, 2⁶³-1] or [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807][ulong][UInt64] (S)unsigned integerUL8 bytes[0, 2⁶⁴-1] or [0, 18446744073709551615][sbyte][SByte] (S)integer 1 byte[-2⁷, 2⁷-1] or [-128, 127][byte][Byte] (S)unsigned integer 1 byte[0, 2⁸-1] or [0, 255][short][Int16] (S)integer 2 bytes[-2¹⁵, 2¹⁵-1] or [-32768, 32767][ushort][UInt16] (S)unsigned integer 2 bytes[0, 2¹⁶-1] or [0, 65535][float][Single] (S)real numberF4 bytes[1,5 10⁻⁴⁵, 3,4 10³⁸] in absolute value, 7 significant digits[double][Double] (S)real numberD8 bytes[5,0 10⁻³²⁴, 1,7 10³⁰⁸] in absolute value, 15 to 16 significant digits[decimal][Decimal] (S)decimal numberM16 bytes[1,0 10⁻²⁸, 7,9 10²⁸] as an absolute value, 28 to 29 significant digits[bool][Boolean] (S)Boolean 1 byte[true], [false][object][Object] (C)object reference reference to an object of any type <p>Above, we have listed the equivalent .NET types for each C# type, marked with (S) if the type is a structure and (C) if it is a class. We see that there are two possible names for a 32-bit integer: [int] and [Int32]. The type [int] is a C# keyword. [Int32] is a structure belonging to the [System] namespace: its full name is [System.Int32]. The type [int] is a C# alias that refers to the structure .NET [System.Int32]. Similarly, the C# type [string] is an alias for the .NET [System.String] type. [System.String] is a class, not a structure. The two concepts are similar, but with the following fundamental difference:</p> <ul> <li>a variable of type structure directly contains its value: this is called a value type;</li> <li>a variable of a class type contains the address of the object (its reference); this is known as a reference type.</li> </ul> <p>In addition, there are two less common types:</p> <ul> <li>[nint] and [nuint] (C# 9) are signed and unsigned integers whose size is that of a memory address: 4 bytes on a 32-bit system, 8 bytes on a 64-bit system. They are primarily used to interact with native code;</li> <li>[System.Half] (.NET 5) is a 2-byte, low-precision floating-point number used in graphics computing and machine learning. It is not a predefined language type: it does not have a C# keyword.</li> </ul> <p>A structure, like a class, is a complex type with properties and methods. Thus, we can write:</p> <pre><code>string? typeName = 3.GetType().FullName;
</code></pre> <p>The literal 3 is by default of type C# [int], and therefore of type .NET [System.Int32]. This structure has a method [GetType()] that returns an object describing the data type. Among the properties of this object, [FullName] is the full name of the type. The literal 3 is therefore a more complex object than it appears at first glance. The question mark in [string?] will be explained a little later.</p> <p>The program [basics-03.cs] illustrates these various points:</p> <pre><code>// the predefined types: .NET type and size in bytes
int integer = 2;
float fl = 10.5F;
double d = -4.6;
string s = "test";
uint ui = 5;
long l = 1000;
ulong ul = 1001;
byte octet = 5;
sbyte so = -5;
short sh = -4;
ushort ush = 10;
decimal dec = 10.67M;
bool b = true;
char c = 'A';
// {0}, {1}, {2} are replaced by parameters no. 0, 1, 2 that follow the format
Console.WriteLine("Type of integer[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", integer.GetType().FullName, integer, sizeof(int));
Console.WriteLine("Type of fl[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", fl.GetType().FullName, fl, sizeof(float));
Console.WriteLine("Type of d[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", d.GetType().FullName, d, sizeof(double));
Console.WriteLine("Type of s[{1}] : [{0}]", s.GetType().FullName, s);
Console.WriteLine("Type of ui[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", ui.GetType().FullName, ui, sizeof(uint));
Console.WriteLine("Type of l[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", l.GetType().FullName, l, sizeof(long));
Console.WriteLine("Type of ul[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", ul.GetType().FullName, ul, sizeof(ulong));
Console.WriteLine("Type of octet[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", octet.GetType().FullName, octet, sizeof(byte));
Console.WriteLine("Type of so[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", so.GetType().FullName, so, sizeof(sbyte));
Console.WriteLine("Type of sh[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", sh.GetType().FullName, sh, sizeof(short));
Console.WriteLine("Type of ush[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", ush.GetType().FullName, ush, sizeof(ushort));
Console.WriteLine("Type of dec[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", dec.GetType().FullName, dec, sizeof(decimal));
Console.WriteLine("Type of b[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", b.GetType().FullName, b, sizeof(bool));
Console.WriteLine("Type of c[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", c.GetType().FullName, c, sizeof(char));
// integer whose size is that of a memory address (4 or 8 bytes depending on the machine)
nint ni = 7;
Console.WriteLine("Type of ni[{1}] : [{0},{2}]", ni.GetType().FullName, ni, nint.Size);
// 2-byte real number (.NET 5)
Half h = (Half)1.5;
Console.WriteLine("Type of h[{1}] : [{0}]", h.GetType().FullName, h);
// the literal 3 is also an object
Console.WriteLine("Type of the literal 3 : " + 3.GetType().FullName);
// extreme values
Console.WriteLine("int : [{0}, {1}]", int.MinValue, int.MaxValue);
Console.WriteLine("long : [{0}, {1}]", long.MinValue, long.MaxValue);
Console.WriteLine("decimal : [{0}, {1}]", decimal.MinValue, decimal.MaxValue);
</code></pre> <p>Results:</p> <pre><code>&gt; dotnet run basics-03.cs
Type of integer[2] : [System.Int32,4]
Type of fl[10,5] : [System.Single,4]
Type of d[-4,6] : [System.Double,8]
Type of s[test] : [System.String]
Type of ui[5] : [System.UInt32,4]
Type of l[1000] : [System.Int64,8]
Type of ul[1001] : [System.UInt64,8]
Type of octet[5] : [System.Byte,1]
Type of so[-5] : [System.SByte,1]
Type of sh[-4] : [System.Int16,2]
Type of ush[10] : [System.UInt16,2]
Type of dec[10,67] : [System.Decimal,16]
Type of b[True] : [System.Boolean,1]
Type of c[A] : [System.Char,2]
Type of ni[7] : [System.IntPtr,8]
Type of h[1,5] : [System.Half]
Type of the literal 3 : System.Int32
int : [-2147483648, 2147483647]
long : [-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807]
decimal : [-79228162514264337593543950335, 79228162514264337593543950335]
</code></pre> <p>Comments:</p> <ul> <li>line 2: declaration of an integer [integer] initialized to 2;</li> <li>line 16: the statement [Console.WriteLine("... {0} ... {1} ...", param0, param1, ...)] displays the text specified as its first parameter (the format) on the screen, replacing each {i} with the value of the i-th parameter following the format. Parameters are numbered starting from 0;</li> <li>line 17: the type of a variable [v] is obtained via [v.GetType().FullName]. The size in bytes of a predefined value type [T] is obtained via [sizeof(T)];</li> <li>line 20: Since the type [string] refers to a class and not a structure, the operator [sizeof] cannot be used;</li> <li>line 33: [sizeof(nint)] is not allowed in regular code because the size of [nint] is only known at runtime. The property [nint.Size] specifies that it is 8 bytes on the 64-bit machine being used;</li> <li>line 35: You cannot write [Half h = 1.5;]: there is no implicit conversion from a [double] to a [Half]. The notation [(Half)] is an explicit conversion (known as a “cast” in English), which we will examine later;</li> <li>lines 40–42: The properties [MinValue] and [MaxValue] of the numeric types specify the bounds of their value domains.</li> </ul> <p>The output displays the type names .NET rather than the C# aliases. Note the decimal comma for floating-point numbers: the numbers are converted to strings according to the system’s locale, in this case French.</p>

DataExamples of literals[int] integer (32 bits)145, -7, 0xFF (hexadecimal), 0b1010_1010 (binary)Integer [long] (64 bits), suffix L100000L, 1_000_000_000_000Lunsigned integer, suffix U or UL4000000000U, 10ULfloating-point [double]134.789, -45E-18 (-45 × 10⁻¹⁸)real [float], suffix F134.789F, -45E-18Freal [decimal], suffix M100000.50Mcharacter [char]'A', 'b', '\n', '\u00e9'string [string]"today", "c:\\chap1", @"c:\chap1", $"{name} is {age} years old", """plain text"""boolean [bool][true], [false]date[new DateTime(1954, 10, 13)] (year, month, day) for 10/13/1954 <p>In the source code, floating-point numbers are always written with a decimal point, regardless of the system’s locale. Since C# 7, integers can be written in binary (prefixed with [0b]) and groups of digits can be separated by the _ character to improve readability: [1_000_000] is the integer one million.</p> <p>A standard string literal can contain escape sequences introduced by the \ character: "\n" represents the end-of-line character and not the sequence of the two characters \ and n, "\t" represents a tab, "\"" represents a double quote, and "\\" represents the \ character itself. To write the Windows path c:\chap1\paragraph3, you must therefore write "c:\\chap1\\paragraph3". You can also write @"c:\chap1\paragraph3": the @"text" syntax specifies that the text should be taken exactly as written, without interpreting the \ character. This is called a verbatim string.</p> <p>C# now offers two other types of strings:</p> <ul> <li>the interpolated string (C# 6) $"...": expressions enclosed in curly braces are evaluated, and their values are inserted into the string. An expression can be followed by a format specifier after the colon: [$"{d1:F2}"] displays the floating-point number [d1] with two decimal places. This is the most readable way to construct a string, and we will use it from now on instead of the {0}, {1}, ... formats;</li> <li>The raw string (C# 11) """...""" : It begins and ends with at least three quotation marks. It can contain quotation marks and unescaped \ characters, and can span multiple lines. Spaces preceding the closing quotation marks are removed from each line, allowing the text to be indented with the code. This is very useful for writing JSON, SQL, or HTML.</li> </ul> <p>The program [basics-04.cs] demonstrates these different notations:</p> <pre><code>// the notation of literal data
// integers: decimal, hexadecimal (0x), binary (0b), separator _
int i1 = 145, i2 = -7, i3 = 0xFF, i4 = 0b1010_1010;
long l1 = 100000L, l2 = 1_000_000_000_000L;
Console.WriteLine("integers : {0} {1} {2} {3} {4} {5}", i1, i2, i3, i4, l1, l2);
// real numbers
double d1 = 134.789, d2 = -45E-18;
float f1 = 134.789F;
decimal m1 = 100000.50M;
Console.WriteLine("reals : {0} {1} {2} {3}", d1, d2, f1, m1);
// characters and booleans
char c1 = 'A', c2 = '\u00e9';
bool b1 = true;
Console.WriteLine("characters : {0} {1}, boolean : {2}", c1, c2, b1);
// a date: (year, month, day)
DateTime birth = new DateTime(1954, 10, 13);
Console.WriteLine("date : {0}", birth.ToShortDateString());

// classic strings: the \ character introduces an escape sequence
string path1 = "c:\\chap1\\paragraph3";
// verbatim string: the text is taken as is
string path2 = @"c:\chap1\paragraph3";
Console.WriteLine(path1);
Console.WriteLine(path2);
Console.WriteLine("line 1\nline 2\t[tab]");

// interpolated string: the expressions between braces are evaluated
string name = "dupont";
int age = 23;
Console.WriteLine($"{name} is {age} years old, in 10 years he will be {age + 10}");
// with a format after the : character
Console.WriteLine($"d1={d1:F1}, i3 in hexadecimal={i3:X}, i4 in binary={i4:B8}, l2={l2:N0}");
Console.WriteLine($"date={birth:dddd d MMMM yyyy}");

// raw string (C# 11): no escaping, no doubling of quotes
string json = """
    {
      "name": "dupont",
      "path": "c:\chap1"
    }
    """;
Console.WriteLine(json);
// interpolated raw string: with $$, the expressions are between {{ and }}
string json2 = $$"""{ "name": "{{name}}", "age": {{age}} }""";
Console.WriteLine(json2);
</code></pre> <p>Results:</p> <pre><code>&gt; dotnet run basics-04.cs
integers : 145 -7 255 170 100000 1000000000000
reals : 134,789 -4,5E-17 134,789 100000,50
characters : A é, boolean : True
date : 13/10/1954
c:\chap1\paragraph3
c:\chap1\paragraph3
line 1
line 2    [tab]
dupont is 23 years old, in 10 years he will be 33
d1=134,8, i3 in hexadecimal=FF, i4 in binary=10101010, l2=1 000 000 000 000
date=mercredi 13 octobre 1954
{
  "name": "dupont",
  "path": "c:\chap1"
}
{ "name": "dupont", "age": 23 }
</code></pre> <p>Comments:</p> <ul> <li>line 3: [0xFF] is 255 and [0b1010_1010] is 170;</li> <li>line 7: -45E-18 is displayed as -4,5E-17: this is the same value, normalized;</li> <li>line 12: the sequence \u00e9 denotes the Unicode character with hexadecimal code 00E9, i.e., é;</li> <li>line 16: a date. The method [ToShortDateString] displays it in the short format used in French culture. For a date without a time, .NET 6 added the type [DateOnly]; we’ll revisit this in Chapter 5;</li> <li>lines 20–22: the two strings are identical, as shown in the output;</li> <li>line 30: an interpolated string. The expressions [name], [age], and [age + 10] are evaluated and then inserted into the string;</li> <li>line 32: a few formats: F1 (one decimal place), X (hexadecimal), B8 (8-digit binary, .NET 8), N0 (thousands separator, no decimal place);</li> <li>line 33: a custom date format: day of the week, day, month in words, year;</li> <li>lines 36–41: a multi-line raw string. Quotation marks and the \ character are written as-is. The four spaces preceding the closing quotation marks have been removed from each line;</li> <li>line 44: an interpolated raw string. With two $ characters, expressions are written between double curly braces {{ and }}: the single curly braces, which appear frequently in JSON, remain plain text.</li> </ul>

ConversionMethodnumber -&gt; string[number.ToString()] or an interpolated string [$"{number}"]string -&gt; int[int.Parse(str)] or [int.TryParse(str, out int number)]string -&gt; long[long.Parse(str)] or [long.TryParse(str, out long number)]string -&gt; double[double.Parse(str)] or [double.TryParse(str, out double number)]string -&gt; float[float.Parse(str)] or [float.TryParse(str, out float number)]string -&gt; decimal[decimal.Parse(str)] or [decimal.TryParse(str, out decimal number)] <p>Converting a string to a number may fail if the string does not represent a valid number. The [Parse] method then generates a fatal error called an exception. This error can be handled by the following [try / catch] clause:</p> <pre><code>try
{
    call the function likely to throw the exception
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    handle the exception e
}
next statement
</code></pre> <p>If the function does not throw an exception, execution proceeds to the next statement; otherwise, it proceeds to the body of the [catch] clause, then to the next statement. We will revisit exception handling in the section dedicated to it.</p> <p>The [TryParse] method (.NET 2.0) avoids the exception: it returns the boolean [true] if the conversion succeeds, [false] otherwise, and stores the resulting number in a variable passed using the keyword [out]. Starting with C# 7, this variable can be declared within the call itself: [int.TryParse(str, out var number)]. The [out] passing mode will be explained in the section on parameter passing. When incorrect input is expected—for example, from a keyboard—[TryParse] is preferable to [Parse]: an input error is not an exceptional situation.</p> <p>The program [basics-07.cs] demonstrates several techniques for converting between numbers and character strings. The function [Display] displays the value of its parameter on the screen:</p> <pre><code>// conversions between numbers and character strings
using System.Globalization;

// data
const int i = 10;
const long l = 100000;
const float f = 45.78F;
double d = -14.98;

// number --&gt; string
Display(i.ToString());
Display(l.ToString());
Display(f.ToString());
Display(d.ToString());

// boolean --&gt; string
const bool b = false;
Display(b.ToString());

// string --&gt; int
int i1 = int.Parse("10");
Display(i1.ToString());
try
{
    i1 = int.Parse("10.67");
    Display(i1.ToString());
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    Display("Error : " + e.Message);
}

// string --&gt; long
long l1 = long.Parse("100");
Display(l1.ToString());
try
{
    l1 = long.Parse("10.675");
    Display(l1.ToString());
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    Display("Error : " + e.Message);
}

// string --&gt; double
double d1 = double.Parse("100,87");
Display(d1.ToString());
try
{
    d1 = double.Parse("abcd");
    Display(d1.ToString());
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    Display("Error : " + e.Message);
}

// string --&gt; float
float f1 = float.Parse("100,87");
Display(f1.ToString());
try
{
    f1 = float.Parse("abcd");
    Display(f1.ToString());
}
catch (Exception e)
{
    Display("Error : " + e.Message);
}

// TryParse: returns false instead of throwing an exception
if (int.TryParse("123", out int i2))
{
    Display($"TryParse(\"123\") succeeded : {i2}");
}
if (!int.TryParse("12x", out var i3))
{
    Display($"TryParse(\"12x\") failed, i3={i3}");
}

// the culture: decimal separator of the current culture
Display($"Current culture : {CultureInfo.CurrentCulture.Name}");
try
{
    d1 = double.Parse("100.87");
    Display(d1.ToString());
}
catch (FormatException e)
{
    Display("Error : " + e.Message);
}
// the invariant culture uses the decimal point
double d2 = double.Parse("100.87", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
Display(d2.ToString());
Display(d2.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
if (double.TryParse("3.14", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out double pi))
{
    Display($"pi={pi}");
}

// a local function (see later) that displays its parameter
static void Display(string s)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"S={s}");
}
</code></pre> <p>Results:</p> <pre><code>&gt; dotnet run basics-07.cs
S=10
S=100000
S=45,78
S=-14,98
S=False
S=10
S=Error : The input string '10.67' was not in a correct format.
S=100
S=Error : The input string '10.675' was not in a correct format.
S=100,87
S=Error : The input string 'abcd' was not in a correct format.
S=100,87
S=Error : The input string 'abcd' was not in a correct format.
S=TryParse("123") succeeded : 123
S=TryParse("12x") failed, i3=0
S=Current culture : fr-FR
S=Error : The input string '100.87' was not in a correct format.
S=100,87
S=100.87
S=pi=3,14
</code></pre> <p>Comments:</p> <ul> <li>line 2: the [System.Globalization] namespace, which contains the [CultureInfo] class, is not included in the implicit using statements;</li> <li>Lines 5–8: the data to be converted;</li> <li>lines 11–14: every object has a method [ToString()] that converts it to a string;</li> <li>lines 25–30: the exception that [int.Parse] may throw is handled. [e.Message] is the error message associated with the [e] exception. The .NET exception messages are in English: .NET 10 does not provide a French translation;</li> <li>line 47: the string uses a decimal comma, in accordance with the system’s French locale;</li> <li>line 73: [TryParse] succeeds and stores 123 in the variable [i2], declared in the call;</li> <li>line 77: [TryParse] fails and returns [false]; the variable [i3] is then assigned the value 0;</li> <li>line 83: the program’s current locale is fr-FR;</li> <li>line 86: In the French locale, the period is not a decimal separator: the conversion fails;</li> <li>line 94: the invariant locale [CultureInfo.InvariantCulture] uses the decimal point, regardless of the machine. It is used to read and write numbers in files or in exchanges between programs, which must not depend on the user’s language;</li> <li>lines 95–96: the same number converted to a string according to the current locale (100,87) and according to the invariant locale (100.87);</li> <li>line 97: [TryParse] also accepts a locale;</li> <li>lines 103–105: a local function. In a high-level programming language, you cannot write methods, since there are no classes; instead, you write local functions, which can be called before they are defined. They are discussed in the section “Local Functions and Tuples.”</li> </ul> <p>The 2008 course stated that real numbers in string form must use a comma: this is true only in the French culture. A program that reads numbers must always know in which culture they were written.</p>
